"A being that is itself alive and at ease.": "daf7c890a4d0",
"A fact about an experience that is closed to you.": "7de913a2d019",
"A friend catches her hand in a door. You see her face tighten, you hear the cry, and you wince. You are entirely sure that she is in pain. Yet you did not feel it. You have never felt anyones pain but your own, and you never will. What reached you was a sound and a movement, and from these you arrived at once, without any sense of reasoning, at a fact about an experience that is closed to you. Philosophers call the puzzle this raises the problem of other minds. It has two parts. The first asks how you know that other people have an inner life at all. The second assumes that they do and asks whether you can know what it is like.": "388df93ecd9a",
"An objective description leaves exactly that out.": "6f32001039a4",
"Behavior has come apart from the usual signs.": "bae4fb15c87f",
"Buddhist philosophers also met the skeptical problem head-on. The Yogācāra school held that what we experience is only mind, and its opponents replied that it must then deny that other minds exist. In the seventh century Dharmakīrti answered in a short treatise, the Proof of Other Mindstreams, and his proof is Mills: I observe that my own speech and action are preceded by intention, and I infer the same of speech and action that are not mine. Four centuries later Ratnakīrti argued that, on Yogācāra principles, the inference fails. The tradition also claims that the gap can be crossed directly. The early texts list knowledge of others minds among the powers that come with deep concentration, and compare it to seeing ones face in a clean mirror. Whatever one makes of that claim, it shows that Buddhism never treated another mind as sealed in principle (Dharma and the West, D11 and D19).": "a239cc934174",
"Descartes saw the gap clearly. Looking down from a window at people passing in the street, he remarks in the Meditations, what he strictly sees is hats and coats, which could conceal automata. He judges that they are men. In the Discourse on Method he asked how one could tell a perfect mechanical imitation of a human being from the real thing, and proposed two most certain tests. A machine could never arrange words so as appositely to reply to what is said in its presence, and it could never act sensibly across all the occurrences of life, because each of its skills would need its own special mechanism. Animals, he concluded, fail both tests and are machines without thought.": "6551b8fea808",
"Eight years later the Australian philosopher Frank Jackson made the point with a human being. Mary is a brilliant scientist who has lived all her life in a black-and-white room and learned about the world from black-and-white screens. Her field is color vision, and she has come to know every physical fact about it: the wavelengths, the cells of the retina, the pathways in the brain, the way people use the word red. One day the door is opened and she sees a ripe tomato. Does she learn anything? Most people answer that she does. She learns what it is like to see red. But she already knew all the physical facts. Jackson concluded that there are facts about experience that physics cannot state, and the argument has been disputed at every step ever since. You can try it below.": "af5934d0f692",
"Hats and coats, which could conceal automata.": "abdc711d6604",
"He has nothing to go on in the hard cases.": "dc3d3ca15140",
"His last line turns on the Chinese word ān.": "a9baadc8f2aa",
"Huizi argues as Nagels reader might: knowledge of an experience requires being the one who has it. Zhuangzi first shows that the principle defeats itself, since Huizi relies on knowing something about Zhuangzis mind in order to state it. His last line turns on the Chinese word ān, which means both how and from where. He answers the second sense. He knows it from where he stands, on a bridge, on a fine day, watching them. The suggestion is that knowledge of another creature is had from within a shared situation, by a being that is itself alive and at ease, and that no proof conducted from outside is needed or possible. Mencius, his contemporary, made a similar observation about human beings. Anyone who suddenly sees a child about to fall into a well feels alarm and compassion, and feels it before any thought of reward. For Mencius that response is the root of all morality.": "343b0732747c",
"Huizi pressed his advantage.": "08f7455202c8",
"Its weakness is that it is an induction from a single case. The lesson on induction showed how little we may infer from one observation. If I had opened one box and found a beetle in it, I would have poor grounds for claiming that every box in the world contains a beetle. And here the conclusion can never be checked, since I cannot open a second box. There is a further oddity. Nobody acquires the belief in this way. An infant responds to its mothers smile long before it could notice any correlation between its own feelings and its own face, which it has never seen.": "81b48e70354d",
"Suppose the first question is settled and other creatures do have experience. In 1974 the American philosopher Thomas Nagel asked how far our knowledge of it can go. He chose bats because they are mammals, close enough to us that few doubt they feel something, and because they perceive the world chiefly by echolocation, a sense we lack. A bat sends out high-pitched cries and builds its picture of the space around it from the echoes. We can describe this process in complete physical detail. What we cannot do, Nagel argued, is know what it is like for the bat. If I imagine hanging upside down and hearing my way through the dark, I learn what it would be like for me to behave as a bat does. The bats own experience has a point of view built into it, and an objective description, which is written from no point of view in particular, leaves exactly that out.": "c36821833335",
"The Buddha uses the argument from analogy, and he uses it for a different purpose. All tremble at the rod, all fear death, says the Dhammapada. Treating others like oneself, neither kill nor incite to kill. The Pali is attānaṃ upamaṃ katvā, literally having made oneself the comparison. In another discourse he teaches householders to reason in this way: I want to live and do not want to die, and others are the same; the thing that is disliked by me is also disliked by others. Mill offered the analogy as a proof that other minds exist, and as a proof it is weak. The Buddha offers it as a practice. He takes the existence of other beings suffering for granted and asks his hearers to keep turning their attention to it until it weighs with them as their own does. The later tradition calls the result the equality of self and other.": "d96211ea306c",
"The classic answer to the first question was given by John Stuart Mill in 1865. I know in my own case, he said, that a burn is followed by pain and that the pain is followed by a cry. I observe that other people have bodies like mine, and that when they are burned they cry out as I do. I conclude that the middle link is present in them as well. This is the argument from analogy, and it describes fairly well what we would say if asked to defend our belief.": "71c9bf3c8836",
"The oldest statement of the problem is a conversation of about sixty words. Zhuangzi and his friend Huizi, a logician, were strolling on the bridge over the Hao River. Zhuangzi remarked that the minnows were swimming about freely, and that this was the happiness of fish. Huizi said: You are not a fish. How do you know the happiness of fish? Zhuangzi replied: You are not I. How do you know that I do not know it? Huizi pressed his advantage. I am not you, so I do not know what you know. You are not a fish, so you do not know the happiness of fish. The case is complete. Zhuangzis answer has been argued over for two thousand years. Let us go back to the beginning. When you asked how I know, you already knew that I knew. I know it from up here on the Hao.": "486cf6887a26",
"The passage is worth rereading today. Descartes was confident that no machine would ever pass his first test, and for more than three centuries he was right.": "4433bd39cda6",
"The problem has left the seminar room. In 2012 a group of neuroscientists meeting at Cambridge signed a declaration that mammals, birds, and other creatures, octopuses among them, have the neural equipment for conscious states, and laws on animal welfare have begun to follow. In hospitals, some patients who show no response at all have been found, when placed in a scanner and asked to imagine playing tennis, to produce the same brain activity as healthy volunteers. And there are now machines that pass the first of Descartess tests with ease. They reply appositely to whatever is said to them, and when asked they will describe their feelings. Whether there is anything it is like to be such a system is a question on which serious researchers disagree, and no agreed method exists for settling it. In each of the three cases behavior has come apart from the usual signs, and we find that we have been relying on resemblance to ourselves without ever deciding which resemblances count.": "659544f3f835",
"They reply appositely to whatever is said to them.": "af228cadafce",
"Wittgenstein thought the whole problem rested on a wrong picture. Imagine, he says, that everyone has a box with something in it that we call a beetle. No one can look into anyone elses box, and each says that he knows what a beetle is only from his own. In that situation the thing in the box would play no part in the meaning of the word. The boxes could hold different things, or nothing. His point is that words such as pain cannot get their meaning from a private inner object that each speaker points to alone. We learn them in public, from people who comfort us when we fall. And in ordinary life we do not infer that someone is in pain. We see it in the face, as directly as we see that the face is pale. On this view the skeptics picture of minds sealed inside bodies is itself the mistake.": "00ae8db284d0",
"You hear the cry, and you wince.": "9dfbdbcc3f8d",
"Zhuangzi and Huizi were strolling on the bridge over the Hao. Zhuangzi said, The minnows swim out and about so freely. This is the happiness of fish. Huizi said, You are not a fish. How do you know the happiness of fish? Zhuangzi said, You are not I. How do you know that I do not know the happiness of fish? Huizi said, I am not you, so I certainly do not know you. You are certainly not a fish, so your not knowing the happiness of fish is complete.": "0b7430188891",
"Zhuangzi said, Let us go back to the root of this. When you said, How do you know the happiness of fish? you asked me because you already knew that I knew it. I know it from above the Hao.": "e264980cc1a8",
